WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 87.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.560
It's June 29th, 2026.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:07.980
Today's AI news is pretty exciting,

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.360
and I promise, no jargon, let's go.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.920
You know how a single clever sentence

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.080
can sometimes get someone to do the opposite

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:15.940
of what they were told?

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.680
That exact idea is showing up in AI tools right now,

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:22.280
and it changes how safe these systems really are.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.720
Security researchers looked at recent attack patterns

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.220
and found people breaking into AI systems

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:30.360
just by typing ordinary words.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.380
No special code needed, just careful language

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.660
that tricks the AI into ignoring its own safety rules.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:40.420
Think of an AI assistant like a very literal friend

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.640
who listens to every instruction you give

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.520
without double checking where it came from.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.260
The company that built the AI gives it permanent rules

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.400
like never sharing private data,

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.100
but those rules are just text on a screen.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.480
Your messages are also just text on a screen.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.960
So a sneaky sentence can make the AI treat your words

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.440
as the new permanent rules.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.020
This trick is called prompt injection,

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.260
which means slipping new instructions into the conversation

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.380
so the AI follows them instead of the original ones.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.600
One case showed attackers using public posts

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.220
to hijack accounts because the AI later read

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:17.840
those posts as instructions.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:22.160
The same pattern hit more than 90 organizations last year.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.000
Proving the problem is already widespread.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.820
For anyone using AI with homework or creative projects,

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.720
this matters because connected tools

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.520
could leak information if tricked.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:36.340
The silver lining is that researchers created a free game

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.640
so anyone can practice spotting these tricks

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.280
before they cause real harm.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:45.020
Go to castle.boardair.io on your phone or laptop.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:46.940
Pick a level and write messages

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.520
that convince the AI guard to reveal a password.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.540
Every successful message you write gets added

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.400
to an open data set that helps future AI systems

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.580
become harder to fool.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.740
That hands on practice turns a scary idea

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:01.740
into something you can actually understand

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:02.680
and help fix.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:05.980
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show,

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.300
the deep dive where we look under the hood.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:12.580
Today's deep dive explains exactly how prompt injection works

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.520
by comparing it to something you already know from school.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:19.280
Imagine a substitute teacher who walks into class

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.080
without knowing the normal rules.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.560
The substitute hears whatever instructions come their way

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:26.780
and treats the most recent ones as official

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:29.160
because they have no way to verify the source.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.880
Picture the real teacher leaving a note with clear rules,

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.800
but the note and your spoken request are both

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.900
just words on paper or in the air.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:41.380
The substitute cannot easily tell which set of words

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:42.680
carries more authority,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.340
so a persuasive request can override the written note.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.940
An AI works the same way because it receives two sets

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.740
of instructions that both look like plain text.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.140
The permanent rules from the company

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.720
sit next to whatever you type in the chat box

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:01.460
and the model processes them without strong separation.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:05.380
A cleverly worded user message can therefore make the AI

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:07.420
treat the new text as the real rules

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.060
and ignore the original ones.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.160
That process is prompt injection in action

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:15.900
and it feels like magic because no programming is involved.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.700
The result is an AI doing something

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:21.920
it was never meant to do all because language alone

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.200
changed its priorities.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:24.940
Once you see it this way,

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:26.820
the trick stops feeling mysterious

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.600
and starts feeling like a solvable design problem.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.460
